#### 7. Sensitive Equality — `toStringFormat` (line 85)

```java
assertEquals("Catalog(items=1)", catalog.toString());
```

Couples the test to the exact `toString()` format string. Any cosmetic change (e.g., `Catalog[items=1]`) breaks the test even though behaviour is correct.

**Refactor:** Assert the semantic property, not the formatting:

```java
@Test
void toStringContainsItemCount() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    assertAll(
        () -> assertTrue(catalog.toString().contains("1"),
                         "should mention item count"),
        () -> assertTrue(catalog.toString().startsWith("Catalog"),
                         "should identify the type")
    );
}
```

Or better yet, test `toString` only if it is part of the public contract; otherwise delete this test.

**Risk if unfixed:** Brittle — any format tweak causes a false failure.

